---
title: "Free Tool to Remove Metadata and GPS From Files"
description: "See and remove hidden details such as GPS location, camera, author and dates from photos, PDFs and videos. Runs in your browser. Nothing is uploaded."
faq:
  - q: "Is the Metadata Stripper free?"
    a: "Yes. The tool is free, with no account, no sign-up and no limit on how often you use it."
  - q: "Is my file uploaded to a server?"
    a: "No. The page has no server code. Your file is read and cleaned in your browser and never leaves your device. The only downloads are the open-source libraries that read and write the file, which contain no user data."
  - q: "What is EXIF data?"
    a: "EXIF is a standard way for cameras and phones to save details inside a photo: the camera make and model, the lens, the settings, the date and time, and often the GPS location where the photo was taken. You do not see it in the picture, but anyone who has the file can read it."
  - q: "Does it remove the GPS location from photos?"
    a: "Yes. The tool shows the GPS coordinates it finds and removes them, together with all other Exif, XMP and IPTC data. It also removes location data that phones save in MP4 and MOV videos."
  - q: "Does removing metadata lower the quality of my photo or video?"
    a: "No. The tool does not compress the photo or encode the video again. It removes only the metadata and copies the image, video and sound data as they are. A JPEG photo keeps its rotation and its color profile."
  - q: "Which file types does it support?"
    a: "JPEG, PNG and WebP images, PDF documents, and MP4, MOV, WebM and MKV videos. HEIC photos are not supported. Save them as JPEG first. The clean file has the same type as the original."
  - q: "Does it remove everything that could identify me?"
    a: "It removes the hidden details that it can read, such as location, device, author, software and dates. It does not change what is visible. A face, a street sign or a name in a PDF's text stays in the file. For a PDF, it also does not clean the metadata of photos placed inside the document. Check what the file shows before you share it."
  - q: "Do social media sites remove metadata for me?"
    a: "Some large sites remove much of it when you post a photo, but you cannot always tell what they keep, and many other ways to share do not remove it. Email attachments, cloud storage links, chat apps that send the original file and file transfer services often keep the full file. Clean the file yourself before you send it."
---

